.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/40nutrb4dq9qp.js\"],\"BiosToggle\"]\n43:I[793915,[\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/35j798tkrmk1i.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/255hn4sxun13n.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/2r35kt0v1clxk.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/38h107cxq9coa.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/3i7-7ioipjh8i.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/2dmsdx6viir09.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/3dr7b776xdda4.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/3rpokotyb5y9l.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/2wqz322vqzqnb.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/3qmiyff324iuh.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/3xqv15cwy7elb.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/3r8m90r-b_gxn.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/42yr50-7e3umv.js\",\"https://journal-assets.ubiquity.press/2.27.0/_next/static/chunks/40nutrb4dq9qp.js\"],\"Bios\"]\n38:[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"className\":\"fPSBzf bYPztT eCASqA hefxLJ hwUhUp bAFlDm dAeywj jvKdHa cqRTIH jnhCGN hKdQOC\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L3c\",null,{\"article\":{\"publication_id\":313,\"submission_id\":256,\"doi\":\"10.5334/irsp.256\",\"url_path\":null,\"date_submitted\":\"2019-01-28T13:18:00+00:00\",\"date_accepted\":\"2019-03-29T08:52:04+00:00\",\"date_published\":\"2019-05-03T11:21:22+00:00\",\"peer_reviewed\":true,\"submission_locale\":\"en\",\"copyright\":\"$8:props:article:copyright\",\"versions\":\"$8:props:article:versions\",\"pages\":\"6\",\"title\":\"Tweeted Anger Predicts County-Level Results of the 2016 United States Presidential Election\",\"thumb\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/jnl-up-j-irsp-public/journals/1/articles/313/submission_256_313_articleThumbnail_en_US.jpg\",\"pdf_link\":\"\",\"html_link\":\"\",\"collections\":\"$8:props:article:collections\",\"taxonomy\":\"$8:props:article:taxonomy\",\"xml_link\":\"\",\"subtitle\":\"\",\"abstract\":\"\u003cp\u003eIn the aftermath of the 2016 United States presidential election, experts and journalists speculated that angry voters had supported the unexpected winner Donald Trump. The present study used a sample of 148 million tweets posted by U.S. citizens from across 1,347 counties, classified with regard to emotional content, to predict the election results at county level. As expected, Donald Trump received more support in counties where people tweeted more anger and negative emotions, even when various county characteristics and conservative vote choice in the preceding presidential election were controlled. These findings might be an outcome of emotional resonance—voters being attracted by political appeals that match their emotions—because Trump used more anger and negative emotion words in his campaign than the other presidential candidates in 2012 and 2016. The findings suggest that negative emotions played a critical role in the 2016 presidential election.\u003c/p\u003e\",\"competing_interests\":\"\",\"cite\":\"\",\"references\":\"\",\"license\":\"$8:props:article:license\",\"keywords\":\"$8:props:article:keywords\",\"authors\":\"$8:props:article:authors\",\"galleys\":\"$8:props:article:galleys\",\"section\":\"Research article\",\"issue\":\"$8:props:article:issue\",\"previous_article_id\":289,\"next_article_id\":277,\"publication_facts\":\"$8:props:article:publication_facts\",\"slug\":\"10.5334/irsp.256\"},\"baseUrl\":\"https://rips-irsp.com\"}]}]\n39:[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"className\":\"fPSBzf bYPztT eCASqA hefxLJ hwUhUp bAFlDm dAeywj jvKdHa cqRTIH jnhCGN\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L3d\",null,{}]}]\n3a:[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"className\":\"fPSBzf bYPztT eCASqA hefxLJ hwUhUp bAFlDm dAeywj jvKdHa cqRTIH jnhCGN\",\"children\":\"$L3e\"}]\n3b:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"ffRpOX hkIdtl BCPsj PYFuN hbybOd fPSBzf fQhpty jWrpcl htvpoH\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"main\",null,{\"tabIndex\":-1,\"className\":\"hlUslA gnlqYH cAAlDe NfyjN cmoMOG\",\"id\":\"main-content\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"article\",null,{\"id\":\"article-container\",\"className\":\"eCkCmc hEdlJD haKexU euBcPj jwbVKN dposoq\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"ljXGdG hIUmpP jrnjrZ gWTXzK cnsCBk iRHjNX\",\"data-no-padding\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"fPSBzf iRgpoQ fXjkDo\",\"children\":[null,null,[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"fPSBzf jaeBJZ\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"jRYqXe kkynes fGeoMI\",\"children\":\"Tweeted Anger Predicts County-Level Results of the 2016 United States Presidential Election\"}],[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"className\":\"gsjAMe jcofsB\",\"children\":\"Research article\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"$L3f\",null,{\"authors\":[\"$8:props:article:authors:0\",\"$8:props:article:authors:1\",\"$8:props:article:authors:2\"],\"limit\":15,\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"fPSBzf iRgpoQ eCANtc frarrB\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"id\":\"article-authors\",\"className\":\"mBgaT gsjAMe\",\"children\":\"Authors\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"fPSBzf dDWCpe gBxyZZ bYPztT jvZfOb\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"fPSBzf iRgpoQ gEYBWy hlUslA\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L40\",null,{\"controller\":\"article-authors\"}],[\"$\",\"$L41\",null,{}]]}],[\"$\",\"$L42\",null,{\"controls\":\"article-authors-bios\"}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"id\":\"article-authors-bios\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L43\",null,{}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"abstract\",\"className\":\"frarrB gsjAMe\",\"children\":\"Abstract\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"eQHHlj erRRVO pvAcl drApKM pSiCW edZasX bInrdi bQDduM jhQjpY ibCwtb jHVsSk hpftSi jYADwv gIYpWW gCaouQ bpiJH iLeIrS euvmnb dWEByn gMTduG kzWhjJ kGrTuD hCoUSX cMEHgi joaZnP ikXHun jYRjMJ IDsss cksOfW jERTDL bTQbBm gCFVTJ jsPgIM fUAnLN dzJchF gCDzvz GHGve yfJWq gpIKX bINCEQ bTrurV iVDmXL gDaDxp iFCSCZ fRdekv fnFPHJ gJNujC eXXgyX bpBxTt eMxTUw dlxxQW dvknbV fFtpRP iBlgIz ibJBrC bobhfO dnRDXP bwXKSp krLtwY bYmdgw eQsVOi itdywl jWLaFk iZJhbs kLUYkr egjWWI kgmHOh jziISh bYOkeh enMKeu eubqet jYoVCR iacOaP HCUCs jLLntq fUakio imSRrt dkXDMO enJcTp iKfJKu gAofhl igxpLC imJVRI klpVDy dsGcTn dwlUMG iDKczH kyIBgn thMLB drLXLT eEhmff bjQUER oCPRg gKcvjY jmLKWF xxWbw hxHqQC iWTHBm evXTJf ezjVPr ggVFqM eFsFfP FEpQK jTudHT\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"In the aftermath of the 2016 United States presidential election, experts and journalists speculated that angry voters had supported the unexpected winner Donald Trump. The present study used a sample of 148 million tweets posted by U.S. citizens from across 1,347 counties, classified with regard to emotional content, to predict the election results at county level. As expected, Donald Trump received more support in counties where people tweeted more anger and negative emotions, even when various county characteristics and conservative vote choice in the preceding presidential election were controlled. These findings might be an outcome of emotional resonance—voters being attracted by political appeals that match their emotions—because Trump used more anger and negative emotion words in his campaign than the other presidential candidates in 2012 and 2016. The findings suggest that negative emotions played a critical role in the 2016 presidential election.\"}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"fPSBzf deTnQX\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"id\":\"article-keywords\",\"className\":\"gsjAMe\",\"children\":\"Keywords:\"}],[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"aria-labelledby\":\"article-keywords\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"li\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"nVRGN kBHUWh\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"clas